#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133616 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ad4e453-00da-5c77-8d6a-5de4dc3d5593" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/id #uuid "4e4236a9-d99f-50b1-803a-ab3aea5a673e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8743" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x^2\\,\\mathbf{j} - 2z\\,\\mathbf{k}$. Evaluate $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $\\left(-1,0,2\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j}+R\\,\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nis a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the curl of $\\mathbf F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ncurlF &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ y & x^{2} & -2z\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= [\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2z) - \\frac{∂}{∂z}(x^{2})]i - [\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2z) - \\frac{∂}{∂z}(y)]j + [\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}) - \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)]k \\\\\n&= (0 - 0)i - (0 - 0)j + (2x - 1)k \\\\\n&= (2x - 1)k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm {curl}\\, \\mathbf{F}$ at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(-1,0,2\\right)\n$$\n\nand obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(curlF)(-1, 0, 2) &= (2(-1) - 1)k =-3k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133620 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d42243ea-58fc-53f6-a0b6-f995bf1d697c" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/id #uuid "cb99a3e1-9cd1-5335-b20c-01ad598d14bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-8929" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$, shown above. Given that $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2132/Source/Images/a7e6add34274406286967ea9c1d41886.png)\n\n1. $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\mathbf 0$\n2. $\\textrm{curl}\\, \\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0$\n3. $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational\n4. $\\textrm{curl}\\, \\mathbf F$ points in the positive $z$-direction." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $\\mathbf F$.\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $\\mathbf F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0$.\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\mathbf 0$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2132/Source/Images/83eaadb4dc59f6f9352c6d5c3d9901b6.png)\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\mathbf 0\n$$\n\neverywhere in $\\mathbb R^3$, we say that $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, whereas statement II is true. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate counterclockwise. Therefore, $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0$.\n- Statements III is false. Since $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0$, the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is not irrotational.\n- Statement IV is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (counterclockwise), our thumb will point upward. Therefore, $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ points upward, in the direction of increasing $z$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133637 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b65b190-b587-5827-a160-b8df85b6227c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/id #uuid "39577555-e9cb-563a-8f5e-8b9ea4b57364" :question/math-academy-id "e-9556" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = e^{x+y} \\, \\mathbf{i} + 3xy \\, \\mathbf{j}$. Evaluate the scalar curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(-1,1)$." :question/worked-solution "If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nis a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3xy) - \\frac{∂}{∂y}(e^{x + y}) \\\\\n&= 3y - e^{x + y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(-1,1)$, and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})_{(-1, 1)} &= 3(1) - e^{-1 + 1} \\\\\n&= 3 - e^{0} \\\\\n&= 3 - 1 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d42243ea-58fc-53f6-a0b6-f995bf1d697c" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d14c0dc6-0dcb-5769-836f-91cc92c4e25d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130468} {:answer/id #uuid "f722db6e-4822-55ec-99f1-c86959ffa315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130469} {:answer/id #uuid "07f7b6c8-ed21-5ddb-a4f5-a02b031e605e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130470} {:answer/id #uuid "40297bbf-017c-577c-b1df-fd9daccbf9a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130471} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd3f194-6e92-5013-bba8-50e9a1607180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07f7b6c8-ed21-5ddb-a4f5-a02b031e605e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130470} :answer-field/id #uuid "15023ceb-01b4-5207-bb54-22d25c31edf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130467}] :question/id #uuid "7a72f589-a1bb-57d0-9bf2-23704868e98f" :question/math-academy-id "q-105092" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2132/Source/Images/q-105092.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x, y, z)$, shown above. Given that $F(x, y, z)$ is independent of $z$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $curlF = 0$\n2. $curlF \\ne 0$\n3. $F$ is irrotational\n4. $curlF$ points in the negative $z$-direction."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ad4e453-00da-5c77-8d6a-5de4dc3d5593" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d283f95-54bf-5543-a3e0-0b16ed1817c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6z\\,j+2\\,\\text{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294436} {:answer/id #uuid "3e7270e5-fb82-52ce-8533-fca2f9a580dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294437} {:answer/id #uuid "c775e906-d35f-59ca-adb9-303e359d87d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6z\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294438} {:answer/id #uuid "28d7e6ed-54b5-5aa5-a4c8-6f7f98fdecd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294439} {:answer/id #uuid "f26d3808-820d-5590-9bfd-c4b888b3180b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6z\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c775e906-d35f-59ca-adb9-303e359d87d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6z\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294438} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1b48c20-cda5-5a3d-88b9-877ca4dcbec0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb2dffd2-9038-550e-ac80-acf29b387b94" :question/math-academy-id "q-105668" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y,z)=3{z}^{2}\\,i+2y\\,j+k,$ what is $\\text{curl}\\,F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 3{z}^{2} & 2y & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{z}^{2})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{z}^{2})]k \\\\  & =(0-0)i-(0-6z)j+(0-0)k \\\\  & =6z\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
